  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 222 AM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nThrough Sunday Morning: \n\nMost of the forecast period will be rather stagnant as the upper low \nassociated with yesterday's trough passage will detach from the flow \nand become cutoff over the Eastern Coast. This in combination with a \nweak split flow upper jet will keep mid to upper level conditions \nfairly similar through the weekend and into next week. While the \nbulk of the forecast will remain similar, slight fluctuations in \nsurface pressure over the next 5-7 days will still lead to slightly \ndifferent temperatures and wind speed/direction over the forecast \nperiod. \n\nCAA will be strong through the morning hours, dropping dew points \ninto the low 30s across northern portions of the area. Even though \nskies are clearing, brisk surface winds of 7-10kt will keep the PBL \nfairly mixed limiting diurnal cooling and saturation enough to \nmitigate frost formation for most areas. Sheltered areas over far NE \ncentral Indiana may see some patchy frost, but this will not be \nabundant enough to warrant headlines. Western portions of the \nforecast areas may fail to fall below 40 this morning as downstream \ncloud cover from Lake Michigan increasing downwelling longwave. \n\nContinued CAA over the next 24 hours will lead to height rises \nbeneath 850mb and therefor building high pressure through most of \ntoday. The centroid of this surface high is expected to overlap with \nthe core of the mid level polar airmass late today and tonight of \nwhich will result in our coldest temperatures of the season. Frost \nlooks definite for most of central Indiana on Sunday morning, but \nthere is still some uncertainty in below freezing temperatures. \nEnsembles are starting to become more aligned on timing of the \nsurface high passage of which should result in below freezing \ntemperatures over northern and northeaster central Indiana tomorrow \nmorning. A Freeze Warning has been issued for these areas, with a \nFrost Advisory for the remaining counties. \n\nRemainder of Sunday through Thursday: \n\nThe surface high will push off to the east on Sunday, allowing for \nweak S/SE flow to develop over the Ohio Valley. This along with \nmostly clear skies will lead to a slight rebound in temperatures \nwith highs on Sunday expected to be in the low 60s. Clear skies and \ndry surface conditions will continue large diurnal swings, but there \nshould be enough of an air mass change to keep temperatures above \nfreezing Sunday night into Monday morning. Frost potential will \nremain high, but headlines will depend if any hard freezes occur \ntonight.\n\nUpper level riding will continue to build throughout the beginning \nof next week leading to subsidence and dry conditions. Continued S/SW \nflow west of the surface high will maintain the steady warming trend \nearly next week as well. 850mb temperatures are expected to increase \nabove 10C by Tuesday. This along with dry adiabatic lapse rates in \nthe PBL should lead to daily temperature above seasonal normals \nMonday through Wednesday. Expect large diurnal swings to continue \nwith lows in the 40s.",
